# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Individual literature-review paragraphs (Section 6), written from the verified extraction."""

PARA = {
"P02": "Esfandyari et al. (2023) modelled an experimental counter-flow concentric double-pipe exchanger (1.2 m, steel) in which hot water or Al2O3/water nanofluid (0.4-0.8 vol%) flowed in the inner tube, optionally under ultrasonic excitation, while the cold annulus stream was fixed at 164 L/h and 20 C. Four dimensional inputs (ultrasound on/off, hot flow 113-257 L/h, hot inlet temperature 40-60 C and volume fraction) were mapped to five thermal outputs - hot-side heat duty, hot- and cold-side Nusselt numbers, NTU and effectiveness - using MLPs (one and two hidden layers, LM/SCG/BR training, three transfer functions), ANFIS (genfis1/genfis2) and PSO-tuned versions of both. The 84 data points were split randomly into 59 training and 25 test points; no validation set was used and the network architecture (best: two hidden layers of 10 LOGSIG neurons) was chosen from average R2, so the test data probably influenced selection. Test R2 of the ANN-PSO ranged from 0.948 (NTU) to 0.999 (cold-side Nu), but test MSE for NTU and effectiveness was 25-65 times the training MSE, the overall R2 for hot-side Nu was printed as 1.976, and effectiveness, NTU and duty were learned as independent targets even though they are linked by the energy balance and the eps-NTU relation. Scaling and the units of the MSE are not reported. Strength: it is the closest experimental analogue to our rig in inputs, fluid and outputs. Weakness: random splitting of a factorial grid measures interpolation only and the model is oversized for 59 training rows. Relevance: primary benchmark for problem formulation, not for validation.",
"P13": "Alsayah et al. (2025) built an ANN surrogate for the overall heat-transfer coefficient U of a counter-flow concentric-tube exchanger from 2,700 axisymmetric CFD simulations (k-omega SST, GCI 0.375%) spanning Re 1,000-20,000 on each side, four air/water fluid pairings, three diameter ratios and lengths of 0.4-4 m; transitional cases were removed. Eleven inputs (both Reynolds and Prandtl numbers, geometry, and conductivities and viscosities of both streams) were standardised. The authors held out 20% as an untouched test set, selected among six MLP architectures with 5-fold CV on the remaining 80%, and retrained the best model (128-256-128 ReLU, L2 = 0.001, Adam lr 0.001, MAE loss, early stopping with patience 200, up to 2,000 epochs; Keras/TensorFlow). The test MAE was 5.5 W/m2K and the MAPE 3.08% (CV validation MAPE 4.96 +/- 0.67%), with regime-wise median absolute errors of 0.1-0.7% against 4.8-25.8% for six classical correlations; SHAP identified cold-side conductivity, hot-side Re, hot-side viscosity and inner diameter as most influential. Strength: the cleanest train/selection/test discipline and physical-unit reporting in the DPHE class. Weakness: noise-free CFD data, random (interpolative) splitting, correlations used without refitting, no nanofluid and no uncertainty. Relevance: secondary benchmark for the ML pipeline and for U as a single learned thermal target; its deep architecture must not be transferred to small experimental data.",
"P37": "Hadj Salah, Ochi and Bezi (2026) generated 200 Latin-hypercube OpenFOAM simulations of laminar Al2O3/water flow in a heated pipe (volume fraction 0.5-5%, Re 1,000-2,000, wall temperature 305-330 K) and 20 independent simulations of an Al2O3-TiO2 hybrid nanofluid represented by mixture-rule properties. An XGBoost model with three inputs predicted Nu; the data were split 80/20 before any preprocessing, hyperparameters were grid-searched by 5-fold CV on the training set only (100 trees, depth 5, learning rate 0.1, L1/L2 penalties), and a leakage-prevention table, fixed seeds and public code are provided. XGBoost reached test R2 0.996 (RMSE 0.045) against 0.989 for an ANN and 0.975 for SVR; fine-tuning the source model on 20 hybrid points gave R2 0.980 (bootstrap 95% CI 0.951-0.988) versus 0.761 from scratch. Uncertainty was quantified with quantile XGBoost (91% coverage of a 90% interval) and a 100-model bootstrap ensemble that was overconfident (72.5% coverage); a retrospective active-learning analysis suggested 40 instead of 200 simulations. Strength: best validation hygiene and the only calibrated uncertainty in the set. Weakness: the hybrid nanofluid exists only through assumed mixture properties, the split is random and several numbers are internally inconsistent. Relevance: supporting benchmark for leakage control, interval calibration and small-data transfer to hybrid nanofluids.",
"P01": "Abdelghany and Sharafeldin (2026) report an experimental and feed-forward ANN study of a plain double-pipe heat exchanger operated with hybrid Al2O3-CuO/water nanofluid. Only the abstract was accessible; inputs, outputs, dataset size, split, architecture and metrics could not be verified. It is retained (Evidence C) as the single exception to the full-text rule because it is the only identified ML study on a hybrid nanofluid in a double-pipe exchanger, which is precisely our configuration. Its methodological value cannot be judged until the full text is obtained; it is therefore not used as a benchmark and is flagged as the highest-priority download.",
"P04": "Sammil and Sridharan (2024) predicted Nu, friction factor, effectiveness and NTU of a copper water-water DPHE without inserts and with gear-disc turbulators (own experiments) and dolphin-ring turbulators (data taken from a previous publication). Three inputs (Re, number of turbulators and turbulator length) were used for 540 rows split 80/20 at random, and random forest, XGBoost and CatBoost were tuned by grid, random and Bayesian search within k-fold CV. CatBoost achieved test R2 0.9989 and AAPRE 0.33%, XGBoost 0.9881 and RF 0.9741. These errors are well below the stated uncertainty of the measured Nusselt number (+/-1.9%), which, together with 540 rows obtained from a handful of operating conditions, strongly suggests that near-duplicate readings appear in both training and test sets. Reporting errors (a learning rate and a fractional min_samples_leaf quoted for RF, inconsistent inlet temperatures and Re ranges, and RMSE values that only make sense on scaled targets) reduce confidence further, and the data are confidential. Strength: a systematic boosting comparison with feature importance. Weakness: implausible accuracy and high leakage risk. Relevance: moderate; it shows what must be avoided when many readings are logged per condition.",
"P05": "Karimpooremam et al. (2025) measured Nu in a horizontal mini double-pipe exchanger with wire-coil inserts (p/d 0-2.4) using Al2O3/water nanofluid (0.02-0.06 vol%, Re 4,000-14,000) as the hot inner-tube stream. Seventy-two Nu values were modelled with a 3-22-1 MLP (inputs Re, volume fraction and p/d; TANSIG/PURELIN; Levenberg-Marquardt) and with two quadratic surface fits. No training/validation/test partition is described; training stopped after 11 epochs with a training MSE of 3.8e-9, and the reported metrics (correlation coefficient 0.983, MSE 11.28, RMSE 3.36 in Nu units, MAE 0.76, deviation within +3.4/-3.2%) therefore appear to describe the fit to the training data. The quadratic fit in Re and p/d achieved RMSE 4.28 with six parameters, so the 111-weight network adds little, and both errors are of the order of the stated Nu uncertainty (+/-3.8%). Strength: physical-unit errors and a polynomial baseline in a nanofluid DPHE. Weakness: no held-out evaluation and an oversized network. Relevance: high for setup, low for method.",
"P06": "Zakeri and Sarmasti Emami (2023) studied a small counter-current DPHE (copper inner tube 8/10 mm, 0.5 m) with deionised water as the hot inner stream (66.7 mL/s) and graphene-oxide nanofluid (0.01-0.1%) in the annulus at 14.4-23.4 mL/s and hot inlet temperatures of 35-55 C. The heat-transfer coefficient was modelled from three dimensional inputs (hot inlet temperature, concentration and cold flow rate) with an MLP, an RBF network and an RSM quadratic model. The data reduction computes the convective coefficients from Sieder-Tate-type correlations with mixture-rule properties, so the learned target is partly a correlation output rather than an independent measurement. The number of runs is not stated; the ANOVA implies about 20. Data were min-max normalised (apparently before splitting) and divided 80/20 at random, leaving about four test points; network architectures are not reported. Test errors were RMSE 5.37 W/m2K (RBF; R2 0.969) and 8.15 W/m2K (MLP; R2 0.93) on an HTC of about 163 W/m2K, while the quadratic RSM reached R2 0.95 (predicted R2 0.91). Strength: inputs closest to our operating variables and physical-unit test errors. Weakness: correlation-derived target, tiny test set and undocumented models. Relevance: high for input design, a warning for target definition.",
"P07": "Sundar, Deepanraj and Mewada (2023) measured h, Nu, effectiveness and NTU in a counter-flow U-bend tube-in-tube exchanger with MWCNT/water nanofluid (0-0.3 vol%) in the inner tube (3-8 L/min, 25 C inlet) and hot water (80 C, 3 L/min) in the annulus, Re 3,751-11,139. Separate Sugeno ANFIS models with fuzzy C-means membership functions used two dimensionless inputs (Re and volume fraction) and an 80/20 random split; the number of data points and the rule structure are not reported. Correlation coefficients of 0.987-0.998 and test RMSEs of 2.5 (Nu, range 20-120), 0.0028 (effectiveness, range 0.46-0.58) and 0.0021 (NTU, range 0.45-0.49) were reported; the abstract presents the correlation coefficients as R2, and both result blocks in the text are labelled 'test data'. Because NTU varies only between 0.45 and 0.49, high correlation is not informative, and effectiveness and NTU are learned independently although they are linked analytically. Multilinear regressions in Re and volume fraction (standard deviation 6.5% for Nu) provide a useful baseline. Strength: effectiveness/NTU targets with dimensionless inputs and physical errors. Weakness: unreported sample size and inconsistent reporting. Relevance: high for target choice.",
"P09": "Meghdadi Isfahani, Hosseinian and Bagherzadeh (2024) predicted the heat-transfer coefficient of a vibrating counter-flow double-pipe exchanger with MWCNT/water nanofluid from flow rate, temperature, concentration and vibration amplitude, comparing a two-layer feed-forward network with a parametric regression fitted by robust least squares. Only the abstract was accessible (Evidence C); dataset size, split and metrics are unknown. It is retained because its operating-variable inputs and regression baseline mirror the structure proposed for our project; its conclusions cannot be weighed until the full text is read.",
"P10": "Krishna Varma et al. (2024) tested a twin-pipe DPHE with cut twisted tapes and Fe3O4/water nanofluid (0.01-0.05 vol%) using a Taguchi L9 array in coolant flow rate, volume fraction and cut radius, and analysed h, Nu, friction factor and thermal performance factor with ANOVA and a feed-forward ANN (one hidden layer of 10, 20 or 30 tansig neurons, Levenberg-Marquardt, MATLAB). The ANN data appear to be the nine L9 runs; the split is not described and the 20-neuron network was chosen because it minimised MSE. Only correlation coefficients are reported (training 1, validation 0.9999, test 1, all 0.9992). A network with roughly one hundred parameters fitted to about nine points cannot provide evidence of predictive skill, and R = 1 on one or two test points is meaningless. Strength: careful experimental uncertainty analysis (Nu 1.64%). Weakness: extreme over-parameterisation and no meaningful validation. Relevance: a negative example despite the closely related set-up.",
"P03": "Celik et al. (2023) re-analysed 162 experiments on a concentric double-pipe exchanger in which air flowed through the inner tube and condensing steam kept the wall near 98 C, with coiled-wire inserts varied in thickness, pitch and length. Four dimensionless inputs (Re, e/d, p/d, l/d) were used to predict Nu, friction factor and thermal performance factor with MATLAB Regression Learner models: robust linear regression, an ensemble labelled 'random forest' whose settings are those of boosted trees, medium-Gaussian SVR, exponential-kernel GPR and a 10-10-10 ReLU MLP, all with presets and evaluated by random 10-fold CV. For Nu the MLP gave RMSE 3.72 (R2 1.00), GPR 8.25 (0.98), SVR 14.97, the ensemble 14.68 and linear regression 19.63; GPR had the lowest RMSE for the friction factor. One- and two-way partial dependence plots were reported, but GPR predictive variance was not used. Strength: the only DPHE-type study comparing five families including GPR and a linear baseline under CV with dimensionless inputs. Weakness: random folds across a 27-geometry x 6-Re grid test interpolation only, the boundary condition (constant wall temperature, air) differs from liquid-liquid nanofluid operation, and the algorithm labelling is inconsistent. Relevance: methodological model for comparing families, not for data.",
"P14": "Kanti et al. (2025) measured Nu, friction factor and entropy generation for water, Al2O3 and Al2O3-CuO (50:50) hybrid nanofluids (0-1 vol%) in a uniformly heated copper tube (Re 7,000-26,444) and modelled them with XGBoost tuned by Bayesian optimisation (for Nu: 456 trees, depth 12, learning rate 0.30). The inputs and the split are not stated; results are shown for six test samples. Training R2 was 1.000 for Nu and test R2 0.975 (MSE 29.5), while the friction-factor model showed a higher test than training R2 (0.916 vs 0.686), an anomaly not explained. A power-law correlation, Nu = 0.11 Re^0.741 Pr^-0.413 (T/60)^-0.56 (1 + phi/100)^51.8, achieved a mean deviation of 3.06% and R2 0.97, essentially matching the boosted model. Strength: experimental hybrid-nanofluid data and a physically structured correlation including a temperature term. Weakness: a very deep booster on a few dozen points, a six-sample test and missing input definitions. Relevance: supports a refitted power-law baseline for hybrid nanofluids.",
"P17": "Gupta et al. (2026) measured Nu and friction factor for GO/water nanofluid (0.025-0.1 wt%) in a tube with wire-coil inserts (P/D 1-2, Re 5,000-18,000) and trained a single 3-12-8-2 feed-forward network (inputs Re, wt% and P/D; tansig/purelin; Levenberg-Marquardt) on 120 data sets after min-max scaling, split at random into 84 training, 18 validation and 18 test samples; the architecture was chosen on validation error. Test R2 for Nu was 0.9912 (validation 0.9869) and for friction factor 0.8728, but RMSE (0.0255) and MAE (0.0211) are only given on the normalised scale. Strength: transparent split counts and architecture selection on validation data. Weakness: no physical-unit error, a random split across a factorial grid, and a joint thermal-hydraulic output layer. Relevance: moderate, mainly as a reporting example.",
"P19": "Celik et al. (2025) analysed a concentric double-pipe exchanger with air in the inner tube, condensing steam outside and corrugated-tape inserts (t/d, w/d, p/d), and regressed second-law and performance quantities (entropy-generation number, exergy destruction, effectiveness and NTU) on Re and the three tape ratios with multiple linear regression, Lasso, Ridge and Elastic Net. Each configuration was run three times and averaged before modelling, which avoids treating repeated readings as independent samples. Lasso and Elastic Net gave R2 about 0.985 and MAPE about 4.1% for effectiveness (linear regression 5.5%) and about 4.9% for NTU, but no train/test partition is reported and adjusted R2 values indicate in-sample evaluation; the measurement uncertainty of the second-law quantities is about +/-11%. Strength: averaging of repeats and regularised linear models suited to small data. Weakness: no held-out validation and an air/steam configuration. Relevance: supports regularised (log-)linear baselines and repeat averaging.",
"P27": "Saraswat (2026) used 24,579 critical-heat-flux measurements from 60 experimental campaigns in heated vertical tubes to test whether ML accuracy survives campaign-disjoint validation. Fifteen pre-crisis inputs (ten dimensionless groups plus five raw variables, after a causal audit that excluded outcome-derived quantities) fed direct HistGradientBoosting and XGBoost regressors and hybrids that learn the multiplicative log-residual of the Bowring correlation; hyperparameters were fixed a priori. The direct learner's MAPE rose from 4.69% under random record splitting to 11.91% when whole campaigns were withheld (leakage amplification 2.54), whereas the correlation-anchored hybrids retained grouped errors of 3.94% and 4.96% (amplification 1.36 and 1.06), outperformed direct learners in 26-28 of 30 held-out partitions, and were insensitive to a 6% bias in the anchor. Queries outside the Mahalanobis-distance domain or the correlation envelope were flagged rather than predicted. Strength: rigorous grouped validation, anchoring and applicability domain. Weakness: boiling CHF physics and a database far larger than ours. Relevance: the methodological template for our validation, anchoring and domain checks.",
"P28": "Wang et al. (2026) compared a dimensionless ANN (inputs: dimensionless groups with property-ratio corrections; output: Nu, from which wall temperature is recovered by Newton's law through a surface-intersection solver) with a parametric ANN mapping mass flow, pressure, heat flux and inlet temperature directly to wall temperature, for supercritical n-decane in a heated thin tube. Both were trained on 410 experimental points and tested on a separate set of 940 points obtained in new experiments extending flow, pressure, heat flux and inlet temperature beyond the training ranges; depth (3-5 hidden layers) and width (10-500 neurons) were explored with tanh activations. Within the training domain both networks performed similarly, but outside it the dimensionless network was more accurate, remained robust when trained on only 200 samples, and never produced non-physical predictions, whereas the parametric network predicted wall temperatures below the bulk temperature. Strength: a genuine out-of-domain experimental test. Weakness: different physics and broad architecture search. Relevance: direct evidence for learning a dimensionless thermal quantity and deriving dimensional outputs through physics.",
"P12": "Colak et al. (2022) generated 438 parametric design cases for multi-hairpin double-pipe exchangers with pure fluids and nanofluids in the tube side, computing the overall heat-transfer coefficient, pressure drops and total cost from standard correlations, and trained two MLPs (one hidden layer of 15 neurons, Levenberg-Marquardt, 70/15/15 random split) with ten inputs (including Reynolds numbers, friction factors and pumping powers) or eight inputs. Deviations of 0.02-0.16% for U and R > 0.98 were reported. Because several inputs are intermediate quantities of the same correlations that generate the targets, the near-perfect accuracy is expected and does not show predictive skill on physical data (a form of illegitimate-feature leakage). Strength: illustrates input redundancy. Weakness: deterministic analytical data. Relevance: cautionary example only.",
"P15": "Sundar, Mewada and Sangaraju (2024) measured thermophysical properties and Nu, h, friction factor and thermal performance factor of rGO/water nanofluids (0.5-2.0 vol%) in a heated tube over Re 1,800-21,000 and modelled each quantity with boosted regression trees and XGBoost using 70 points per target (56 training, 14 test; inputs not listed explicitly). For Nu, BRT reached training/test R2 0.999/0.89 and XGBoost 0.999/0.991, with test RMSE 11.6 and 6.7 Nu units respectively; hyperparameters are not reported and several MAE values contain obvious typographical errors. The near-zero training error against much larger test error indicates overfitting, and laminar, transitional and turbulent data are pooled. Strength: experimental nanofluid data with train/test/all metrics in physical units. Weakness: overfitting and incomplete reporting. Relevance: low-moderate.",
"P16": "Akilu et al. (2024) measured Nu and friction factor of SiO2 nanofluids in glycerol and ethylene glycol (up to 1.0 vol%) in a small heated stainless tube and modelled Nu from Reynolds and Prandtl numbers with linear regression, decision tree, random forest, XGBoost and AdaBoost (Python; random 70/30 split; hyperparameters not reported). XGBoost had the lowest test error (R2 0.987, MAPE 0.59%), followed by random forest (0.98, 0.71%) and the decision tree (0.972, 0.91%, with zero training error), whereas untransformed linear regression gave R2 0.35. A test MAPE below the uncertainty of the measured properties (k 2.4%, viscosity 4.1%) is difficult to reconcile with genuine generalisation, and a log-linear Nu = c Re^m Pr^n model - which the authors present elsewhere in the paper - would have been the appropriate baseline instead of an untransformed linear model. Strength: dimensionless inputs and a five-model comparison. Weakness: doubtful test independence and a straw-man baseline. Relevance: low-moderate.",
"P18": "Alharthi et al. (2024) measured Nu and friction factor for Al2O3/water nanofluid (0.3-0.9 vol%) in electrically heated conically coiled tubes (coil torsion 0.02-0.052, Re 4,836-12,562) and trained feed-forward networks in MATLAB with mass flow rate, concentration and coil torsion as inputs, using a 70/15/15 random split. The network is described as having three hidden layers with PURELIN (linear) transfer functions, which makes it a linear model; neuron counts and the number of samples are not given. Nu was predicted with R 0.9952, MSE 9.62, RMSE 3.10 and MAPE 2.6% (the abstract interchanges MSE and RMSE). The paper then presents predictions at 0.5% and 0.8% concentration - never measured - as results. Strength: clearly stated inputs and physical errors. Weakness: contradictory network description, missing sample size and unvalidated interpolated predictions. Relevance: low-moderate; shares authors with P01.",
"P21": "Mousavi et al. (2025) simulated a double-pipe exchanger with helical annulus fins (Fluent, k-omega SST) at 44 RSM-designed combinations of Re, fin number, fin height and twist pitch, and trained separate 4-7-1 networks for Nu and friction factor after min-max scaling, using a 39/5 split and 9-fold CV with a 'total goodness function' that combines training and test performance to select the number of neurons. R2 close to 1 was reported, sensitivity analysis ranked Re first for Nu, the network weights and biases were published, and the GA optimum was re-simulated. Strength: reproducible published weights and a confirmation run. Weakness: deterministic CFD data and test data used in model selection. Relevance: low for prediction, useful for reproducibility practice.",
"P22": "Sheikhi Azizi et al. (2024) simulated a DPHE with twisted tri-lobe tubes at 41 RSM cases varying both Reynolds numbers (10,000-25,000), both twist ratios and twist direction, and trained four single-hidden-layer networks (Nu and f on each side) on 36 cases with five test cases and no validation set, tuning them with a goodness function that includes the test data; RSM quadratic models performed worse. A GA then searched an extended domain (Re 5,000-50,000, twist ratio 2.5-25); new CFD runs at the AI-guided points confirmed the trends but the authors acknowledge a discrepancy between predicted and simulated objective values attributed to extrapolation. Strength: one of the few studies that actually re-simulates extrapolated predictions. Weakness: deterministic data, five test points and test-informed tuning. Relevance: evidence that accuracy degrades outside the training domain.",
"P26": "Syed (2025) studied water/graphene nanofluids (0.1-0.5 vol%) in a heated tube with convergent and convergent-divergent nozzle inserts, trained SVR and random forest on about 220 CFD samples (80/20 split) and checked the models against ten experimental samples; separate models were built per concentration, so concentration is not a learned input. Experimental validation gave Nu R2 0.97-0.99 for RF and 0.91-0.96 for SVR. The rig's water validation showed mean errors of +/-28.5% for Nu and +/-18.5% for friction factor against literature data, which weakens the experimental anchor, and hyperparameters are not reported. Strength: the idea of validating a simulation-trained surrogate on independent experiments. Weakness: weak rig validation and minimal ML reporting. Relevance: low.",
"P29": "Loyola-Fuentes et al. (2024) proposed a six-step framework for ML regression of heat-transfer data (pre-treatment, feature selection, data-splitting philosophy, training/testing, grid-search tuning and performance assessment with MAE and the percentage of points outside +/-20%) and applied it to 4,333 condensation heat-transfer coefficients in microfin tubes with random forest and three deep networks (TensorFlow) against a semi-empirical correlation. With a random split the best ML model achieved MAE 535 W/m2K versus 1,061 for the correlation, but when 40 tube geometries were held out entirely the ML error rose to 1,819 W/m2K whereas the (un-refitted) correlation gave 1,111 W/m2K; expert-knowledge features improved accuracy. Strength: an explicit comparison of interpolation and unseen-geometry performance. Weakness: two-phase flow and a large literature database. Relevance: strong evidence that random splits overstate ML performance and that physics-based correlations extrapolate better.",
}
